 "channel": "philosophy",
 "style": "ps2",
 "title": "BRAIN IN A VAT",
 "angle": "If your whole world reached you as signals on a wire, could you ever prove it is real?",
 "answer": "No test from inside can prove it; Putnam only showed that a brain envatted since birth could never truly say it is in a vat, and that trick fails for a brain moved there last night.",
 "logline": "Cells in a dish played Pong through pulses, so could you prove your world is real? Descartes feared a demon, so Putnam made it a computer feeding a brain in a vat, but every test you run is fed in too, because the computer sends the pinch, as the Pong cells learned from signals without knowing a game existed, but Putnam found a way out in words, because a word only points at what it is connected to, so a lifelong vat-brain's 'I am in a vat' is false, but that only works from birth, so a brain moved last night would notice nothing, so you cannot prove it.",
 "hook_text": "BRAIN CELLS, NO BODY, PLAYING PONG",
 "hooks": [
  "Your brain has never seen the world. It only gets signals.",
  "A philosopher proved you can't say you're a brain in a vat. Almost."
 ],
 "world": {
  "scenery": "room"
 },
 "script": [
  {
   "text": "In 2022, 800,000 brain cells in a dish learned to play Pong.",
   "say": "In twenty twenty-two, eight hundred thousand brain cells in a dish learned to play Pong.",
   "beat": "hook"
  },
  {
   "text": "The ball reached them only as electrical pulses.",
   "beat": "hook",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So if your world arrived as pulses, could you prove it's real?",
   "beat": "question",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "The worry goes back to 1641: Descartes feared a demon was fooling his senses.",
   "say": "The worry goes back to sixteen forty-one: Day-cart feared a demon was fooling his senses.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "answer"
  },
  {
   "text": "Hilary Putnam's 1981 version swaps the demon for a computer, feeding a brain in a vat.",
   "say": "Hilary Putnam's nineteen eighty-one version swaps the demon for a computer, feeding a brain in a vat.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "But you can't test your way out.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "Pinch yourself, and the computer simply sends the pinch.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "because"
  },
  {
   "text": "The Pong cells were taught this way: a hit brought a steady signal, a miss brought noise.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "They improved without ever knowing there was a game.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "But Putnam found a stranger way out: words.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "An ant whose trail looks like Winston Churchill hasn't drawn him; it has no link to him.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So a lifelong vat-brain has never met a real vat; its word vat means images.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "So when it thinks, I am a brain in a vat, the thought comes out false.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "But the trick only works if you've been in the vat since birth.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "A brain moved there last night still means real vats, and would notice nothing.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So no, you can't prove it; you can only prove a lifelong vat-brain couldn't say it.",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "answer"
  },
  {
   "text": "But those Pong cells never knew they were in a dish. Would you?",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "but"
  }
 ],
